import{t as e}from"./utils-COoNfFoj.js";var t={slug:`bytesize`,influencerName:`ByteSize`,influencerHandle:`@ByteSize`,influencerAvatarUrl:`${e}/bytesize-avatar.png`,heroDescription:`Experts Exchange built this to turn fragmented weekly internet reliability signals, including outages, SSL/TLS failures, DNS issues, and anomalies, into a clear operational summary teams can act on quickly. Pairing source-backed incident tracking with newsletter-ready reporting helps technical audiences understand what broke, why it mattered, and which trends deserve attention.`,assetTitle:`Experts Exchange`,screenshotUrl:`${e}/bytesize.png`,remixUrl:`https://www.perplexity.ai/computer/a/experts-exchange-reliability-r-Cl4UxOAiSYG3TK6vKt.nLA`,promptTitle:`Try ByteSize's prompt`,promptText:`Build a dashboard that aggregates weekly internet reliability data including outages, SSL failures, and DNS issues, and outputs a clean summary with the top incidents, trends, and notable anomalies formatted for a newsletter.
- Data source inventory: Identify public weekly reliability signals for outages, SSL/TLS failures, DNS issues, and notable incidents, prioritizing machine-readable or stable sources.
- Data model and aggregation: Define incident categories, severity scoring, time windows, source attribution, trend calculations, and anomaly flags.
- Dashboard implementation: Build a polished web dashboard with KPI cards, incident ranking, trend charts, anomaly callouts, filters, and a newsletter-ready summary panel.
- Newsletter formatting: Generate a clean weekly digest format with top incidents, trend bullets, and anomaly notes that can be copied directly into a newsletter.
- Validation and deployment: Test the dashboard, verify citations/source links for displayed facts, and deploy it to a private accessible URL.

The dashboard functionality is already built. Do not rebuild the product from scratch or change the core functionality, information, or logic. I want you to redesign the visual presentation so it feels custom to my brand and not like a generic default dashboard. This is a creative wrap / styling pass on top of the existing dashboard.

What the dashboard already does: Surfaces real-time reliability signals across Experts Exchange content (verified answers, expert validation, engagement quality); highlights human vs. AI contrast, reinforcing the value of expert-verified solutions; tracks solution quality metrics like acceptance rate, comments, and peer validation; provides a trust score / reliability layer that can be applied to content, topics, or datasets; acts as a proof point for enterprise buyers, demonstrating why EE data reduces hallucinations and improves AI outputs.

Brand vibe: Trusted Technical. Signal > Noise. Human-verified. Enterprise-grade.

Reference links (strong comps for feel, not exact product): Stripe (clean, technical, premium data feel); Palantir (data intelligence + seriousness); Vercel (modern dev-focused UI); Linear (minimal, high-performance product feel); Notion (structured clarity, modular UI).

Uploaded images: Use visuals that reinforce credibility + technical depth + real-world usage — screenshots of Experts Exchange solution threads (blurred + highlighted accepted answers), close-ups of code snippets, configs, terminal outputs, dashboard-style visuals (graphs, metrics, uptime charts), subtle textures (dark UI panels, grids, data overlays), enterprise environments (server rooms, network diagrams, cloud infrastructure visuals), “human layer” imagery (expert reviewing content, annotating, collaborating), clean UI blocks showing “Verified Answer” / “Accepted Solution” tags, and ByteSize newsletter snippets to show reach + authority.

This dashboard should feel like a mission-critical intelligence layer for AI — where every signal is verified, every answer is accountable, and enterprises can trust the output. Be mindful that the formatting of this dashboard should be different from the base structure that Computer initially gives. Do not just restyle the default layout superficially. Push the composition, hierarchy, spacing, grouping, framing, and visual rhythm so it feels more custom, distinctive, and brand-native while still preserving usability and the existing functionality. Keep the content and utility intact, but make the final presentation feel visually original, creative, and specific to my brand world rather than a standard SaaS admin panel.`};export{t as default};
